    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The largest empire in West African history, Songhai controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger Bend</a> and its trade cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>.</li><li>Its wealth came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">trans-Saharan gold</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a>, and its patronage made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> a centre of Islamic scholarship.</li><li>Weakened by civil war, it was shattered in 1591 by a small Moroccan army whose firearms routed a far larger Songhai force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Tondibi</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a>, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> around 1510, reported that books were sold there at greater profit than any other merchandise.</p></aside>
    </section>

